Body:
These behaviors are available through separate V2 APIs:

```ts
// Admit a normal user message without starting the agent loop.
await client.session.prompt({
  sessionID,
  text: userText,
  resume: false,
})

// Admit model-visible synthetic context without starting the agent loop.
await client.session.synthetic({
  sessionID,
  text: context,
  resume: false,
})

// Attach durable, session-scoped system instructions.
await client.session.instructions.entry.put({
  sessionID,
  key: "kimaki",
  value: system,
})
```

`resume: false` is the V2 equivalent of admitting input without requesting a reply. Instruction-entry changes apply at the next step boundary. The 8 KiB limit is per entry; larger instruction sets can be split across stable keys such as `kimaki.000`, `kimaki.001`, etc.

`session.command` immediately executes a slash-command callback rather than admitting a prompt, so prompt admission and system context are handled through the APIs above.

Closing as covered by the current V2 API.

Ran into this same issue using Granite 4.2 on a local Ollama setup, so wanted to chime in in case it's useful.

Looking at the Granite 4.2 docs, the think parameter accepts not just true/false but also level strings ("low"/"medium"/"high"): https://ollama.com/library/granite4.2

Not sure if this is already on your radar, but it might make sense for think to live at the model level instead of the provider level (next to baseURL), following the standard format from Ollama's own API spec — something like:

```
jsonc
{
  "provider": {
    "ollama": {
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://.....:11434",
        "options": {
            "think": "medium"
          }
      },
      "models": {
        "granite4.2:8b": {
          "options": {
            "think": "medium"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Would be great if this could tie into the /thinking toggle fix so it maps directly to this field. Thanks for working on this!

Confirmed on macOS with `opencode2 v0.0.0-beta-17519`.

I built a local idle-notification plugin under `~/.config/opencode/plugins/`. The V2 plugin loaded successfully and appeared in `GET /api/plugin`, but `ctx.event.subscribe()` never delivered a `session.status` event during multiple completed agent turns.

This documented event-subscription approach did **not** work:

```ts
setup(context) {
  const controller = new AbortController()

  void (async () => {
    for await (const event of context.event.subscribe({ signal: controller.signal })) {
      if (event.type !== "session.status" || event.data?.status?.type !== "idle") {
        continue
      }

      await playSound()
    }
  })()

  return () => controller.abort()
}
```

The plugin’s `setup()` did run, and `afplay /System/Library/Sounds/Glass.aiff` worked from that process, so this was not a plugin-loading or macOS-audio issue. The legacy V1 event path was also ineffective in this V2 session.

Working workaround:

```ts
const waiting = new Set<string>()

await ctx.session.hook("context", (event) => {
  if (waiting.has(event.sessionID)) return
  waiting.add(event.sessionID)

  void ctx.session
    .wait({ sessionID: event.sessionID })
    .then(playSound)
    .catch(() => {})
    .finally(() => waiting.delete(event.sessionID))
})
```

`context` fires reliably, and the detached `ctx.session.wait()` resolves when the complete agent turn becomes idle. This allowed the notification to work without relying on the silent event stream.

This supports the report’s core finding: accepted V2 plugins can have a functional `setup()` and hooks while `ctx.event.subscribe()` remains a silent no-op. A stable session-settled/idle plugin hook would avoid requiring this workaround.

